  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 204758732800
    m_Localized: The nucleus is delimited by the membrane called the nuclear envelope,
      which has pores that allow communication with the cytoplasm, as well as connection
      with the Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum. Inside the nucleus are the chromosomes
      and one or more nucleoli. Chromosomes are formed by DNA molecules that are
      wrapped in proteins. Nucleoli are structures where the accumulation of ribosomal
      RNA (rRNA) and transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules occurs, after they are synthesized,
      along with proteins. The rRNA, after being transported to the cytoplasm, will
      form the ribosomes. When the cell is not dividing, the chromosomes appear as
      tangled masses of long, thin DNA filaments dispersed in the nuclear fluid,
      constituting the so-called chromatin. The condensed portions of the chromatin
      make up the heterochromatin, while the less condensed regions make up the euchromatin.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1
    m_Localized: The rough endoplasmic reticulum is a system of channels bounded
      by lipoprotein membranes in the form of sacs and flattened vesicles that communicate
      with the nuclear envelope. It has ribosomes attached to the membrane surface
      facing the cytoplasm. Its functions include the intracellular transport of
      proteins synthesized by ribosomes, as well as modifications that some proteins
      may undergo before being sent to the Golgi complex.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2
    m_Localized: The smooth endoplasmic reticulum is a system of tubular channels
      bounded by lipoprotein membranes, continuing from the rough endoplasmic reticulum.
      It does not have attached ribosomes. Its functions include intracellular transport
      of substances, lipid synthesis, and detoxification of substances harmful to
      the cells.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3
    m_Localized: The centriole is a cylindrical structure consisting of bundles of
      three parallel microtubules. Centrioles are absent in prokaryotes and flowering
      plants, while in animals, two perpendicular centrioles are usually present.
      After duplicating, they guide the formation of spindle fibers during cell division.
      They also play a role in the formation of cilia and flagella
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4
    m_Localized: 'Mitochondria function in producing energy through the process of
      aerobic cellular respiration. They have two membranes: an outer smooth and
      continuous membrane, and an inner membrane, which is folded due to invaginations
      that form the mitochondrial cristae. The inner membrane delineates the interior
      of the mitochondria, known as its matrix, where mitochondrial DNA, enzymes,
      and other matrix components are immersed.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 5
    m_Localized: The Golgi apparatus is composed of a system of membranous vesicles,
      along with smaller spherical vesicles that form from the larger ones. Its membranes
      are lipoproteic and never have attached ribosomes. Its functions are related
      to the storage and routing of proteins produced in the rough endoplasmic reticulum.
      The smaller vesicles transport the synthesized macromolecules to other locations,
      inside or outside of the cells. Polysaccharide synthesis also occurs in the
      Golgi apparatus, with some of them being attached to proteins to form glycoproteins.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6
    m_Localized: Lysosomes originate from vesicles released by the Golgi apparatus.
      They generally have a spherical shape and are composed of a membrane enclosing
      hydrolytic enzymes. Their function is related to intracellular digestion, both
      of food particles ingested by the cell and of worn-out or damaged cellular
      components.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 7
    m_Localized: Nucleus
